      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "My apologies for the confusion. Let's get straight to the point. The straight-line depreciation method is calculated by subtracting the salvage value from the initial cost of the asset and then dividing that amount by the useful life of the asset.\n\nHere's the formula for straight-line depreciation:\n\nAnnual Depreciation Expense = (Cost of Asset - Salvage Value) / Useful Life of Asset\n\nFor your equipment:\n\nCost of Asset = $150,000\nSalvage Value = $25,000\nUseful Life of Asset = 10 years\n\nNow, let's plug in the numbers:\n\nAnnual Depreciation Expense = ($150,000 - $25,000) / 10\nAnnual Depreciation Expense = $125,000 / 10\nAnnual Depreciation Expense = $12,500\n\nSo, the annual depreciation expense for your equipment is $12,500. You can use this figure in your financial reports to reflect the straight-line depreciation of the asset over its useful life.",
